If you're using pine nuts: In a #small skillet{} over medium-low heat, warm the @pine nuts{1 1/2%tablespoons} until they're fragrant and turning lightly golden on the edges, about ~{3-5%minutes}, stirring often (don't let them burn!). Transfer them to a #bowl{} to cool.

In a #shallow serving bowl{}, combine the @cherry or grape tomatoes{1%pint}(halved or quartered if large), @oil-packed sun-dried tomatoes{1/4%cup}(thinly sliced), @fresh basil{1%tablespoon}(thinly sliced) and @garlic{1%medium clove}(pressed or minced). Add @olive oil{1%tablespoon} and @thick balsamic vinegar or balsamic glaze{1%teaspoon}, and stir to combine all of the ingredients.

Gently tear open each ball of @burrata{8%ounces} and nestle them gently into the center of the tomato mixture. Drizzle the burrata with the remaining @olive oil{1%teaspoon} and @thick balsamic vinegar or balsamic glaze{1%teaspoon}. Sprinkle the pine nuts all over the dish, followed by some reserved basil leaves, plus a sprinkle of @freshly ground black pepper{}. Sprinkle a pinch of @?flaky sea salt{} over the cheese, if using.

Serve with @?crostini{} or crusty bread{} on the side, if using.

> This burrata is best the day it's made, but leftovers are good for several days in the refrigerator, covered.